b/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446370 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb932edd-7a39-5566-85fa-5d17f1b76d5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and  $\\text{rank}(A)=4?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "007b2eff-be4f-5ff5-af75-29d7ce49d108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446371 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ is an invertible matrix because $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is invertible, it must be $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{nullity}(A)=0$ because $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a0c9232-89fc-57dc-83d9-632995f2a765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446372 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20d9e520-65af-5666-8bc0-5f07c585e966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446373 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Col}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=0?$ \n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40fb1ecb-b40f-5f25-b77a-57f70d15c44b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446374 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col)}(A)=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null)}(A)=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. A matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=0,$ our matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\nSince $A$ is not invertible, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible,  it must be $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66aeec20-f33b-58e9-909c-e866d7b79c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446375 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "783f1132-a5b8-51d7-9eb1-23f2ac5cdb40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446376 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)={R}^{6}$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "833e6fc8-0df5-5faa-ab1e-b5f4126e71c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446377 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A$ is an $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Null}(A)={R}^{6}.$* $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nIII. $det(A)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9326523-4bce-5554-9699-fb91223b9531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446378 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$ then  $A$ is invertible.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$  then according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$ then  $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is *not* invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ which means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ actually has a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$ then  $det(A)=0.$* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible it must be $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7063fa7-cfd5-5cea-b242-ca27bb233616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446379 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{rank}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $det(A)=0$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec73c900-b909-5aa7-beeb-5f5b6ee2fba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446380 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $det(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then we must have $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$ * $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
